body:
## Problem

In the id web world page, clearing the **World seed** field silently installs/compiles the module with seed `0` instead of reporting an error — in both the install and the compile flows.

## Evidence (verified on main f7dfea35)

- The seed input is rendered with a preset value (`pkgs/id/src/web/templates.rs:793`):
  `<input data-world-seed ... type="number" value="1" aria-label="World seed">`
- Both handlers parse it the same way (`pkgs/id/web/src/world.ts:342-346` for install, `pkgs/id/web/src/world.ts:464-468` for compile):

  ```ts
  const seed = Number(moduleSeed.value);
  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(seed) || seed < 0) {
    setStatus("Seed must be a non-negative safe integer", "error");
    return;
  }
  ```

- `Number("") === 0` and `Number("  ") === 0` (verified with node), and `Number.isSafeInteger(0)` is `true` — so an emptied (or whitespace-only) field passes the guard and the request goes out with `seed: 0`.
- The seed is not cosmetic: the host starts the program from `init(seed)` (`pkgs/id/src/world.rs:583`), and the install seed is sensitive enough that join projections deliberately zero it (#240, `pkgs/id/src/world.rs:820-837`). Silently falling back to the predictable seed `0` when the user cleared the field (intending the preset `1`, or no seed decision at all) installs a world whose hidden state derives from a seed the admin never chose.

No existing issue covers this: search for seed-related issues returns #240 (seed redaction, closed), #556/#543/#534 (unrelated world features).

## Why it matters

- Silent wrong-value bug: the UI appears to validate the seed (it rejects negatives, fractions and unsafe integers) but misses the most common invalid state — an empty field.
- The same defect exists in both flows (install and compile), consistent with the duplicated parsing.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Clearing the seed field and clicking Install shows "Seed must be a non-negative safe integer" (or an equivalent explicit empty-seed error) and sends no `install_begin` message.
- [ ] Same for the Compile flow: no `compile` message is sent with an empty seed.
- [ ] Valid seeds (including an explicit `0` typed by the user) still work in both flows.
- [ ] A regression test covers the empty-seed case (e.g. by extracting the parse into a shared, testable helper used by both handlers — which also removes the duplication).

## Suggested fix

Parse from the raw string and reject empty input before conversion, in one shared helper used by both handlers:

```ts
function parseSeed(raw: string): number | null {
  const trimmed = raw.trim();
  if (trimmed === "") return null;
  const seed = Number(trimmed);
  return Number.isSafeInteger(seed) && seed >= 0 ? seed : null;
}
```

## Bug

`id(web)`: `format_unix_timestamp` converts a timestamp to a calendar date by looping **one year at a time from 1970**, with no range check — and the timestamps it formats come from unvalidated tag data. A single file carrying a huge `modified` / `created` / archive timestamp makes every file-list render spin for minutes to effectively forever.

`pkgs/id/src/web/templates.rs:915-971` (on main 402b5aa1):

```rust
fn format_unix_timestamp(ts: u64) -> String {
    // ...
    let mut y: i64 = 1970;
    let mut remaining = days_total as i64;
    loop {
        let days_in_year: i64 = if leap { 366 } else { 365 };
        if remaining < days_in_year { break; }
        remaining -= days_in_year;
        y += 1;
    }
    // ...
}
```

The iteration count is `(year - 1970)`: ~54 for a 2024 timestamp, ~8,029 for year 9999 — and ~5.8×10¹¹ for `u64::MAX`.

## Where the unvalidated values come from

The only caller is the file-list renderer (templates.rs:294-296): `display_ts = file.modified_at.or(file.created_at).or(file.timestamp)`. All three are attacker/buggy-peer-influenceable `u64`s with no range validation:

- `modified_at` / `created_at`: parsed at routes.rs:781-796 from the tag store's `modified` / `created` tag values with a bare `s.parse::<u64>()` — any value that parses is accepted.
- `timestamp`: from `classify_tag` (routes.rs:150-168) — a file named `x.archive.18446744073709551615` yields `timestamp = u64::MAX`.

Contrast `parse_iso_timestamp` in the same codebase, which was already hardened (#756) to reject years outside 1970..=9999 precisely because unrepresentable dates must not roll through the arithmetic. The render path never got the same guard.

## Verified this run

Standalone replication of the year loop (same structure as templates.rs:930-943):

- Normal timestamps are instant (2024 → 54 iterations; year 9999 → 8,029).
- Cost grows linearly with the year (final full probe run, optimized `rustc -O` build of the loop): 2024 timestamp → 561 ns; a `ts` of 10¹⁵ s (~year 31.7 million, 3.17×10⁷ iterations) → 85.8 ms; a `ts` of 10¹⁷ s (~year 3.17 billion, ~3.2×10⁹ iterations) → **7.23 s for a single conversion**.
- `u64::MAX` needs ~5.85×10¹¹ iterations ≈ 22 minutes at the measured rate (~4.4×10⁸ iterations/s) — for practical purposes the request never finishes. Because the tag is persisted, **every** subsequent file-list render hangs the same way — a stored, repeatable hang, not a one-off slow request. (Correction comment on the issue records these final numbers; an interim wrapper timeout had suggested the 10¹⁷ case never finished.)

## Why it matters

The file list is the web UI's home view. One malformed or malicious metadata tag — synced from a peer, imported, or set by a buggy client — permanently wedges it for that store, with no error and no log pointing at the cause. Even non-adversarial values far outside the plausible range (e.g. millisecond timestamps stored as seconds, ~10¹²–10¹³) waste real time on every render as the year count grows.

## Acceptance criteria

- `format_unix_timestamp` (or its callers) never loops proportionally to the year for out-of-range input: clamp/reject timestamps outside a documented plausible range (e.g. the 1970..=9999 range `parse_iso_timestamp` already uses), rendering an empty/placeholder date instead.
- Or replace the year loop with closed-form civil-date arithmetic (constant time for any `u64`).
- A file with `modified=u64::MAX` (and one named `x.archive.18446744073709551615`) renders the file list promptly with a sane placeholder.
- Regression tests cover `u64::MAX`, a millisecond-scale value, year 9999 boundary, and normal timestamps producing unchanged output.
- Targeted test path: `cargo test --features web --lib web::templates` on CI or the laptop — not a sandbox build.

## Suggested fix

Short term: in the renderer, skip formatting when `display_ts` is outside `0..=253402300799` (9999-12-31) — mirroring the #756 range — and format nothing (the existing `unwrap_or_default()` empty-date path already handles absence). Longer term, closed-form days→civil conversion removes the linear loop entirely.

## Bug

`id(web)`: saving a code/config (Raw mode) file inserts a spurious newline wherever the editor split a `code_block`'s text into multiple text nodes — the same multi-node split the markdown codec was already fixed for (#882), in the one save path that fix missed.

`pkgs/id/src/web/routes.rs:2042-2065` (on main 402b5aa1), `extract_raw_text` — used by `convert_doc_to_bytes` for `ContentMode::Raw` (routes.rs:1977-1980), reached from both save paths (routes.rs:1201, 1761):

```rust
for node in content {
    if node_type == "code_block" {
        for inline in inline_content {
            if let Some(text) = inline.get("text").and_then(|t| t.as_str()) {
                parts.push(text.to_owned());
            }
        }
    }
}
parts.join("\n")
```

Every text node becomes a separate `parts` entry and the entries are joined with `"\n"`. A text-node boundary inside a ProseMirror `code_block` carries no meaning — editors/collab split blocks into several text nodes routinely (that is exactly the premise of #882, whose fix in `json_to_ast`, markdown.rs, concatenates **all** text nodes with `collect::<String>()` and no separator).

## Verified this run (faithful transcription probe of the function)

- Single text node `"let x = 1;\nlet y = 2;"` → saves as `let x = 1;\nlet y = 2;` (correct).
- The identical block split into two text nodes `"let x"` + `" = 1;\nlet y = 2;"` (no newline at the split) → saves as `let x\n = 1;\nlet y = 2;` — a newline inserted mid-statement.
- Same content, different saved bytes, purely as a function of how the editor happened to chunk the text nodes. Split inside a string literal or a line-continued expression, this corrupts the file on save; everywhere else it silently reformats code the user did not touch.

## Why it matters

Raw mode is how code and config files are edited in the web UI. A save that rewrites untouched lines — inserting newlines at invisible node boundaries — produces phantom diffs and can break files whose syntax is newline-sensitive at the split point. The markdown save path already treats multi-node code blocks correctly (#882); the raw path is the inconsistent remainder.

## Acceptance criteria

- Text nodes **within** one `code_block` are concatenated with no separator (matching the #882 fix in `json_to_ast`); multiple `code_block`s in one doc keep a single newline between blocks.
- A doc whose code block is split into multiple text nodes saves byte-identically to the same doc with a single text node.
- Regression tests cover: split mid-line, split inside a string literal, and the existing single-node / multi-block cases.
- Targeted test path: `cargo test --features web --lib web::routes` on CI or the laptop — not a sandbox build.

## Suggested fix

In `extract_raw_text`, build one string per `code_block` by concatenating its text nodes, push that per-block string into `parts`, and keep `parts.join("\n")` for block separation only.

## Problem

`needsRefresh()` in `pkgs/id/web/src/main.ts:291-296` silently suppresses identity-token refresh forever when the stored refresh timestamp is not numeric:

```ts
function needsRefresh(): boolean {
  const refreshedAt = localStorage.getItem(IDENTITY_REFRESHED_AT_KEY);
  if (!refreshedAt) return true;
  const elapsed = Date.now() - Number(refreshedAt);
  return elapsed >= IDENTITY_REFRESH_INTERVAL_MS;
}
```

`Number("garbage")` is `NaN`; `Date.now() - NaN` is `NaN`; `NaN >= interval` is `false`. So any non-numeric value in `id_identity_refreshed_at` (corrupt storage, a value written by another build/version, manual tampering) makes `needsRefresh()` return `false` on every SPA navigation, indefinitely — the exact path (`main.ts`, `onMainSwapped`) whose comment says it "keeps the 30-day token alive" never calls `ensureIdentity()`.

Verified with node on main 4f66baae: `Date.now() - Number("garbage") >= 86400000` → `false`. A future/garbage-but-numeric timestamp has the same shape: a far-future value yields a negative elapsed and also suppresses refresh until wall-clock time catches up.

## Why it matters

The failure mode is silent and total for the affected browser profile: the token is never proactively refreshed, so the user is eventually logged out at the 30-day expiry despite using the app daily via SPA navigation. `localStorage` is untrusted, user-writable state — parsing it with a bare `Number()` and no finiteness check is the same silent-reinterpretation family the id web code has been fixing elsewhere (#707/#832 world-seed, #712 goto-line), but here the bad value disables a keep-alive instead of producing a visible error.

## Acceptance criteria

- A non-numeric or non-finite stored timestamp is treated as "needs refresh" (`true`), as is a timestamp implausibly far in the future.
- Normal behavior unchanged: missing key → `true`; elapsed ≥ 24h interval → `true`; recent timestamp → `false`.
- The check is covered by a test (extracting the parse into a small testable helper next to the other `pkgs/id/web/src/*.test.ts` suites is fine), or an equivalent verified repro is attached to the fixing PR.
- Single-repo: `needsRefresh`/`IDENTITY_REFRESHED_AT_KEY` exist only in developing-today/code `pkgs/id/web/src/main.ts`.

## Problem

`isLightColor` in `pkgs/id/web/src/cursor-utils.ts:66-73` misclassifies CSS 3-digit shorthand hex colors as dark, no matter how light they are:

```ts
export function isLightColor(color: string): boolean {
  const hex = color.replace("#", "");
  const r = parseInt(hex.slice(0, 2), 16);
  const g = parseInt(hex.slice(2, 4), 16);
  const b = parseInt(hex.slice(4, 6), 16);
  const luminance = (0.299 * r + 0.587 * g + 0.114 * b) / 255;
  return luminance > 0.5;
}
```

For a 3-digit input the slices misalign: `"fff"` → r=`"ff"`=255, g=`"f"`=15, b=`""`=`NaN`, so the luminance is `NaN` and `NaN > 0.5` is `false`.

## Verified (verbatim function, node, on main 4f66baae)

- `isLightColor("#fff")` → `false` (white — expected `true`)
- `isLightColor("#ff0")` → `false` (yellow — expected `true`)
- `isLightColor("#000")` → `false` (correct only by accident)
- Any non-hex input (e.g. `"red"`, `"#gggggg"`, `""`) also silently yields `NaN` → `false`, with no distinction from a genuinely dark color.

## Why it matters

`isLightColor` picks the label text color for remote cursors at `pkgs/id/web/src/cursors.ts:352` and `cursors.ts:431` (`isLightColor(cursor.color) ? "#000" : "#fff"`). A cursor color arrives from peers/server via cursor updates (`cursors.ts:826`, `color: cursorUpdate.color`) and is never validated to be 6-digit hex. A peer whose color is shorthand white/yellow gets white text on a white/yellow label background — the name label is illegible. The existing tests (`cursor-utils.test.ts:162-203`) cover only 6-digit forms (with and without `#`), so the gap is invisible to CI.

## Acceptance criteria

- `isLightColor("#fff") === true`, `isLightColor("#ff0") === true`, `isLightColor("#000") === false` — 3-digit shorthand is expanded per CSS rules (each digit doubled) before parsing.
- Malformed/non-hex input has a defined, tested behavior (e.g. treated as dark) instead of falling out of a `NaN` comparison by accident.
- New cases added to `cursor-utils.test.ts`; the existing cursor-utils suite still passes.
- Single-repo: the only copy of this helper is in developing-today/code `pkgs/id/web` (checked the other three repos' clones — no other `isLightColor`).

## Problem

In the id web UI, **any** file whose name starts with `auto-` is classified as an auto-backup, even when the rest of the name is not a timestamp at all. A user's ordinary file named e.g. `auto-notes.txt`, `auto-2026-report.md` or `auto-draft` is treated as `FileKind::Auto` instead of `FileKind::Primary`.

## Evidence (verified on main aa853c40)

`pkgs/id/src/web/routes.rs:150-157` — `classify_tag` returns Auto unconditionally on the prefix; the parsed timestamp is discarded-or-None with no gate:

```rust
fn classify_tag(name: &str) -> (FileKind, Option<u64>) {
    // Check for auto-backup: "auto-{ISO_DATE}"
    if let Some(iso_str) = name.strip_prefix("auto-") {
        // Try to parse the ISO date to extract a unix timestamp
        // Format: 2026-03-12T06:42:30.015Z
        let ts = parse_iso_timestamp(iso_str);
        return (FileKind::Auto, ts);   // Auto even when ts is None
    }
    ...
```

The doc comment on `FileKind::Auto` (routes.rs:114) defines the kind as matching `auto-{ISO_DATE}` — a name whose remainder does not parse as an ISO date does not match that pattern, yet is classified Auto anyway.

Existing tests cover only the well-formed case (`test_classify_tag_auto`, routes.rs:2424-2428, `auto-2026-03-12T06:42:30.015Z`). There is no test for an `auto-` name with a non-date remainder. (Contrast the `.archive.` branch: its no-timestamp behavior is deliberate and pinned by `test_classify_tag_archive_no_timestamp`, routes.rs:2438-2443 — this issue is only about the `auto-` branch, which has no such pinning test or comment.)

## Why it matters

Classification drives user-visible behavior for a misclassified primary file:

- **Sorting**: the file list sorts primaries first (alphabetically), then Auto files by timestamp descending (`get_file_list`, routes.rs ~786-806). A misclassified file drops out of the alphabetical primary list into the backup group, with `timestamp: None` sorting as 0.
- **Badge**: the file list renders an `auto: {parent}` badge for Auto files (templates.rs:232-238); with no same-hash primary, `parent_name` is `None` and the badge renders literally as `auto: ?`.
- **Parent resolution**: only Primary names populate the hash→primary map (routes.rs ~733-736), and `get_file_name` "preferring primary names" (routes.rs ~884-910) will not prefer the misclassified file's name for its hash.

`auto-` is a plausible prefix for real user files (auto-generated reports, `auto-save` notes, vehicle/insurance documents, etc.), so this is not a theoretical name shape.

## Suggested fix

Only classify as Auto when the remainder actually parses:

```rust
if let Some(iso_str) = name.strip_prefix("auto-") {
    if let Some(ts) = parse_iso_timestamp(iso_str) {
        return (FileKind::Auto, Some(ts));
    }
    // fall through: an `auto-` name that is not an ISO date is a primary file
}
```

Note: this composes with #756 — once `parse_iso_timestamp` validates ranges, names like `auto-2026-13-40T99:99:99Z` also stop being Auto, which is the correct outcome under the same rule.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] `classify_tag("auto-notes.txt")`, `classify_tag("auto-2026-report.md")` and `classify_tag("auto-")` return `FileKind::Primary` with `None` timestamp.
- [ ] `classify_tag("auto-2026-03-12T06:42:30.015Z")` still returns `FileKind::Auto` with `Some(ts)` (existing test keeps passing).
- [ ] New regression tests cover the non-date `auto-` shapes; the `.archive.` branch behavior is unchanged.
- [ ] Web unit tests pass (`cargo test` for the web routes module).

## Problem

Several stacked PR Nix checks fail in `checks.x86_64-linux.test-e2e` while Rust lint/format/build checks pass. The Firefox Playwright suite reports unrelated browser/web failures across runs:

- file search/clear and hash-redirect tests observe stale files from other tests;
- blob requests fail with `socket hang up`;
- autosave tests observe duplicate save requests;
- other basic/editor/image tests fail in the same Firefox run.

Observed on PR #224 run 38889252869 (12 failed, 2 flaky, 116 passed) and PR #225 run 38889332765 (11 failed, 2 flaky, 117 passed), with the corresponding #226 flake check also failing. These profile/provenance changes do not touch the web E2E suite.

## Follow-up

Investigate whether the Firefox suite shares a test server, file/blob store, or browser context across parallel workers; isolate per-test state and/or stabilize server teardown/retry behavior. Re-run the full Nix check after the fix. Until then, distinguish these suite failures from the targeted Rust validation for the stacked PRs.

## Finding

`QueueClient.ensureWorker()` in pm-dispatch memoizes its registration promise with `??=` — including when that promise **rejects**. One transient registration failure (a PostgREST 5xx, a network blip) therefore wedges every subsequent launch for the life of the process: each later `ensureWorker()` returns the same rejected promise and throws the original, stale error.

## Evidence

- PR #2655 (commit `88e2e699e1`, MERGED) introduced `ensureWorker()`.
- Verified on current main (`c4fb599617166d4c705eabeb34442ca179ecb9a6`), `pm-stack/dispatch/src/queue.ts:132,137-158`:

  ```ts
  private workerEnsured: Promise<void> | null = null;

  ensureWorker(): Promise<void> {
    this.workerEnsured ??= (async () => {
      const res = await fetch(`${this.baseUrl}/rest/v1/workers`, { … });
      if (!res.ok) {
        const text = await res.text();
        throw new QueueError(`worker registration failed (HTTP ${res.status}): …`, res.status);
      }
    })();
    return this.workerEnsured;
  }
  ```

  There is no `.catch` that resets `workerEnsured` to `null`, and no other code path clears it.
- Caller order makes it worse: `launchSession` (`src/server.ts:116-129`) creates the OpenCode session and sends the prompt **before** `ensureWorker()` runs. So every launch after the first failure creates a real, running session, then throws at registration — and on the `wait:true` path the task is cancelled and the launch reported FAILED while that session keeps working, untracked.

## Why it matters

Registration is a one-time, idempotent upsert whose failure modes are overwhelmingly transient — this exact class of failure is what the fleet hit repeatedly on 2026-10-30 (PostgREST 503s during the Supabase outage). A retry-on-next-launch costs nothing; a permanently poisoned cache turns a blip into a dispatch outage that only a service restart fixes, plus a trail of untracked sessions.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A rejected registration attempt is not cached: `workerEnsured` is reset on rejection so the next `ensureWorker()` call retries the upsert (a *successful* registration stays cached).
- [ ] A unit test covers: first attempt fails (non-OK response or fetch throw), second attempt succeeds, and subsequent calls reuse the success without re-fetching.
- [ ] No other behaviour of `ensureWorker()` changes (same worker row, same ignore-duplicates upsert).

Found by the pr-diff-review job reviewing PR #2655 (batch 2630–2658, 2026-10-30). Deduped: `gh issue list --search 'ensureWorker worker registration dispatch'` and `'workerEnsured'` (open + all) returned nothing covering this.

## Problem

In pm-dispatch's queue client (`pm-stack/dispatch/src/queue.ts`, main 732ca26d), `QueueClient.ensureWorker()` memoizes its registration promise permanently — including when it rejects:

```ts
private workerEnsured: Promise<void> | null = null;   // queue.ts:132

ensureWorker(): Promise<void> {
  this.workerEnsured ??= (async () => {
    const res = await fetch(`${this.baseUrl}/rest/v1/workers`, { method: "POST", /* … */ });
    if (!res.ok) {
      const text = await res.text();
      throw new QueueError(`worker registration failed (HTTP ${res.status}): …`);
    }
  })();
  return this.workerEnsured;                          // queue.ts:137-158
}
```

The `??=` assignment happens once, with the in-flight promise. If that first attempt fails — one transient Supabase/PostgREST 500, one network blip at service start — the rejected promise is cached for the life of the process, and every later `ensureWorker()` call rethrows the original error **without making a new request**. Since `launchSession` (`src/server.ts:130`) awaits `ensureWorker()` before the claim PATCH, every subsequent `POST /v1/sessions` launch fails at that step until the service is restarted — even though the worker-row upsert is idempotent (`resolution=ignore-duplicates`) and a retry would succeed.

## Evidence (verified this run)

Laptop probe (bun, importing the real `queue.ts` from the laptop primary at main 732ca26d, md5-identical to the sandbox clone; probe at `~/git/fleet/probe-2026-10-30-laneB-dispatch/` on the laptop), with global `fetch` stubbed to return HTTP 500 on the first call and HTTP 201 thereafter:

```
B ensureWorker: fetchCalls= 1 firstErr= worker registration failed (HTTP 500): boom secondErr= worker registration failed (HTTP 500): boom
```

One fetch was ever made; the second call rethrew the cached rejection instead of retrying.

## Why it matters

pm-dispatch is a long-lived service. A single transient failure during its first launch permanently wedges all session launches (the service's primary endpoint) behind an error message that points at a failure that is no longer happening — the worst kind of stale error to debug. The enqueue-only `/v1/tasks` path keeps working, so the service looks half-alive.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A failed `ensureWorker()` attempt is not cached: the next call retries the registration request (e.g. clear `workerEnsured` on rejection, or only memoize after success).
- [ ] A successful registration is still memoized (no per-launch POST).
- [ ] A regression test with a fail-then-succeed stubbed transport pins both behaviors (fetch call counts: 2 across fail+retry, 1 across two successes).
- [ ] Existing dispatch tests still pass.

Relocated from dezren39/nix#355 on 2026-10-28 (placement audit): mcpx's single source lives in developing-today/code (pkgs/mcpx); re-home tracked at #309. Original body below; issue references inside it are historical (dezren39/nix numbering).

## Summary

Tool filters (`tools`, `extraIncludeTools`, `excludeTools`; #351, #352) decide by **tool name**: a tool is either callable with any arguments or not callable at all. Many tools are safe with some arguments and unsafe with others, and the choice today is to give up the tool or accept its worst case:

| tool | safe | unsafe |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `github.create_repository` | `private: true` in a known organization | `private: false`, or no `organization` (lands in a personal account) |
| `github.push_files`, `create_or_update_file`, `delete_file` | a feature branch | `main` |
| `datadog.execute_datadog_workflow` | a few reviewed workflow ids | any workflow |
| any tool taking `owner`/`org` | the caller's own organizations | anyone's |

This adds **argument rules**: per tool, and per argument across tools, a JSON Schema constraint that a call's arguments must satisfy, or mcpx refuses the call before it reaches the server.

## Depends on

- **#254**, JSON Schema validation. Rules are schemas, so they need a real validator (2020-12 by default, draft-07 and 2019-09 as servers use them, local `$ref` only). Shipping this first would mean a second partial validator alongside `internal/mcpspec`'s test subset. #254 lands the validator, and both upstream `inputSchema` validation and these rules use it.

Related: #114 (tool views: pin, rename, narrow a schema; this is its "narrow a schema" and part of "pin an argument"), #340 (ask/allow/deny write policy; see below), #257 (annotation trust: rules are keyed by name and arguments, never by server-supplied hints).

## Proposal

A per-server `argumentRules` map in the `mcpx` block, also allowed in the top-level `pool` block. **Keys** are tool patterns, matched exactly as `tools`/`excludeTools` match: exact name, glob (`delete_*`), or `/regexp/`. **Values** are JSON Schema objects. A call is allowed only if its arguments validate against every rule whose key matches the tool.

```jsonc
"github": {
  "mcpx": {
    "argumentRules": {
      // Per function: the whole argument object.
      "create_repository": {
        "required": ["private", "organization"],
        "properties": {
          "private": { "const": true },
          "organization": { "pattern": "^(my-org|my-org-sandbox)$" }
        }
      },
      // Several functions, one rule.
      "/^(push_files|create_or_update_file|delete_file)$/": {
        "required": ["branch"],
        "properties": { "branch": { "not": { "enum": ["main", "master"] } } }
      },
      // Per argument, across every tool that has it: `properties` applies only
      // when the argument is present, so tools without an `owner` are untouched.
      "*": {
        "properties": { "owner": { "pattern": "^(my-org|my-user)$" } }
      }
    }
  }
},
"datadog": {
  "mcpx": {
    "argumentRules": {
      "execute_datadog_workflow": {
        "required": ["workflow_id"],
        "properties": { "workflow_id": { "enum": ["<id-1>", "<id-2>"] } }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Per function and per argument are the same mechanism: a per-argument rule is a schema with `properties` under a key matching many tools. No second syntax is needed. A shorthand could come later if the `properties` wrapper proves noisy.

### What a constraint can say

Anything JSON Schema says, including:
- `const`, `enum`, `not`
- `pattern` (regular expressions; see the dialect note under Limits)
- `minimum`/`maximum`, `maxLength`, `maxItems`
- `required`
- `if`/`then` across arguments, e.g. "if `organization` is absent, refuse"
- `items` for array arguments, e.g. every path in `push_files.files[*].path` under `docs/`

### Semantics

- **Where it is checked:** in `resolveAndGuard`, the step every tool call path shares (`Call`, `CallReporting`, `CallAsk`). It runs after the hidden-tool check and before `elicit.confirmDestructive`, and before the pool is touched, so a refused call starts nothing.
- **Refusal:** 400, as a hidden tool is. The message names the argument path (`/organization`), what was sent, the constraint, and where the rule came from (config file, server, key). It's rendered like `mcpx diagnose` output, so an agent can correct itself.
- **Combining:** every matching rule applies (`allOf`). Pool and server rules add up, and so do rules from layered config files: rules only tighten, and a nearer file cannot loosen a farther one's. That's the same reasoning as `excludeTools`.
- **Advertising:** the effective schema is the upstream `inputSchema` `allOf` every matching rule. It's what `mcpx types`, the generated TypeScript client, search, and the MCP pass-through's `tools/list` show. An agent sees `private: true` in the types before it ever calls, and `mcpx diagnose`/the pre-run check refuse a script that cannot satisfy it without running it.
- **Validation of the rules themselves:** a value that is not a valid schema, or a `pattern` that does not compile, is a configuration error naming the server and key, the same as a bad tool pattern today.
- **Dead rules:** a key matching no tool, or a `properties` entry naming an argument no matched tool has, is a warning at load and on every catalog change. A server that renames `organization` to `org` must not silently disable the rule. `catalog-history` already detects the schema change and can say which rule it orphaned.
- **Server defaults are invisible:** a schema constrains what is sent, not what the server fills in. `create_repository` defaults `private` to `true` today, but a rule that relies on that is one server release from wrong. Constraints that matter should list the argument in `required`. The docs should say so plainly.

### Relationship to #340 (ask/allow/deny)

An argument rule is a deny with a condition. #340 needs the same condition for "ask before `execute_datadog_workflow` unless the id is one of these". Design the two together:

- **Shared:** one rule shape, `{ tool pattern, when: <schema>, action: deny | ask | allow }`. `argumentRules` is the deny-only form, and can be implemented first as exactly that.
- **Separate:** whatever ends up in the config, the evaluator is one function so the two can never disagree.

### Possible follow-up: pinning (#114)

`"set": { "private": true }` would inject a value when the caller omits it and refuse a different one. It's useful but changes what reaches the server, so it should be a separate step with its own tests, after deny-only rules are solid.

## Limits

- **Pattern dialect.** JSON Schema `pattern` is ECMA-262. If #254 picks a Go RE2-based validator, lookaround and backreferences are unsupported. A rule using them must be a load-time error, not a pattern that silently never matches.
- **Free-form arguments.** A `query`/`jql`/`sql` string can be pattern-matched but not truly constrained. `pattern: "env:production"` does not stop `OR env:*`. The docs should say that a tool whose danger lives in a free-form argument belongs in `excludeTools`, not in a rule.
- **Equivalent arguments.** Some servers accept the same thing two ways, such as `owner` vs `organization`, or an id vs a URL. A rule must cover each spelling, and the dead-rule warning cannot detect a missed synonym.
- **Arguments are not effects.** A rule constrains the request mcpx forwards. It does not stop a server from doing something its arguments did not say.

## Acceptance

- Unit tests:
  - tool-pattern keys
  - `allOf` composition across pool, server and layered files
  - schema validity and pattern-dialect errors
  - dead-rule detection, including after a catalog change
- Registry test over all three call paths: a violating call is refused before the pool is touched; a conforming one reaches it. That's the shape of `TestAHiddenToolCannotBeCalled`.
- An e2e test through the real daemon and fake server: refused and allowed calls, the 400 body naming the argument path, and the tightened schema in `mcpx types` and the generated client.
- A pass-through test: an MCP client sees the tightened `inputSchema` in `tools/list` and gets an error result, not a transport failure, on a refused call.
- Falsification for each mechanism, recorded in the commit body: disable the check, the composition, and the advertising in turn, and show the test that fails.
- Docs: a section beside "Hiding tools" in `docs/configuration.md`, with the server-default and free-form-argument cautions.

## Open questions

1. **Name:** `argumentRules`, `argumentConstraints`, or rules folded into a single `policy` block shared with #340.
2. **Per-argument shorthand:** should `"args": { "owner": {...} }` exist as sugar for `"properties"`, or is one syntax better?
3. **Refusal on the pass-through:** an MCP client gets a tool result with `isError: true` and the explanation (agent-readable, recommended) or a JSON-RPC error.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Blocked on #254: a shared JSON Schema validator (2020-12 default; draft-07/2019-09; local `$ref` only) exists and is used by both upstream `inputSchema` validation and argument rules
- [ ] `argumentRules` is accepted in per-server `mcpx` blocks and the top-level `pool` block; keys match exactly as `tools`/`excludeTools` do (exact, glob, `/regexp/`)
- [ ] A call is refused before reaching the server when its arguments fail any matching rule, with a refusal that names the rule; calls matching no rule are untouched
- [ ] The worked examples from the proposal (create_repository private+org pin, branch-not-main, owner allowlist, workflow-id allowlist) are covered by tests, including the per-argument `*` form applying only when the argument is present

## Finding

PR #479's `bin/muse-proxy.mjs` pins the Muse sandbox's network path into every child process, unconditionally, on any machine:

```js
const EGRESS_PROXY = 'http://hatch-egress-proxy:3128';
const EGRESS_CA = '/run/hatch/egress-tls/ca-bundle.pem';
...
const childEnv = () => {
  const env = { ...process.env };
  delete env.META_API_KEY;
  env.HTTPS_PROXY = EGRESS_PROXY;
  env.HTTP_PROXY = EGRESS_PROXY;
  env.https_proxy = EGRESS_PROXY;
  env.http_proxy = EGRESS_PROXY;
  env.SSL_CERT_FILE = EGRESS_CA;
  return env;
};
```
(bin/muse-proxy.mjs:29-30 and 141-148 at PR head 9e7dcbde15e35bd71b7e50dd5762285477255c65; the spawn site is line 264.)

`hatch-egress-proxy` is a sandbox-internal hostname and `/run/hatch/egress-tls/ca-bundle.pem` a sandbox-only path. On any non-sandbox machine (a laptop, another dev box — the natural places to run a `bin/` proxy from this repo), every spawned `muse exec` therefore gets an unresolvable proxy and a nonexistent CA file, so the CLI cannot reach Meta — and this overrides even a correct proxy/CA configuration the operator deliberately set in the server's own environment.

The PR body already identifies this ("Known sandbox-ism: the child env pins the sandbox's unauthenticated egress proxy + CA bundle … Making that conditional (only when those paths exist) is a follow-up, deliberately not bundled here"), but that follow-up was never filed: issue searches for "hatch-egress", "egress proxy muse", "18747", and "muse-proxy" return only #480 (the separate warm-session follow-up, which IS filed) and unrelated proxy issues. This issue files the promised follow-up so it is not lost when #479 merges.

## Evidence (reproduced against the PR head)

Ran the PR's `bin/muse-proxy.mjs` with a stub `muse` that dumps its environment, with the server started as:

```
HTTPS_PROXY=http://operator-proxy.example:8080 SSL_CERT_FILE=/operator/ca.pem META_API_KEY=should-be-stripped
```

The child process environment contained:

```
HTTPS_PROXY=http://hatch-egress-proxy:3128
https_proxy=http://hatch-egress-proxy:3128
SSL_CERT_FILE=/run/hatch/egress-tls/ca-bundle.pem
```

i.e. both operator values were silently replaced by the sandbox constants. (`META_API_KEY` was absent, as intended — that deletion is deliberate and documented, and is not part of this finding.)

Cross-repo check: `git grep muse-proxy` on the current mains of developing-today/opencode, developing-today/openchamber, and dezren39/nix is empty — this file exists only in developing-today/code, so this is a single-repo issue.

## Why it matters

The other `bin/*-proxy.mjs` bridges in this repo run wherever the operator runs them. A bridge that only works inside one specific sandbox — and fails by overriding the operator's working network configuration rather than by an explicit error — will present as "muse is down / auth broken" on any other machine, with the actual cause buried in a child process's environment.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] The sandbox egress proxy + CA are applied to the child env only when they are actually usable (e.g. the CA file exists and/or the proxy host resolves), as the PR body proposed.
- [ ] Values the operator set in the server's environment (`HTTPS_PROXY`/`HTTP_PROXY`/`SSL_CERT_FILE` and lowercase variants) are not overridden when the sandbox pin is not applied.
- [ ] The `META_API_KEY` deletion is preserved (it is load-bearing: the key would outrank the CLI's OAuth login and route to the unfunded pay-as-you-go wallet).
- [ ] A test or documented manual check covers both cases: sandbox-shaped environment (pin applied) and plain environment (operator env passes through untouched).

