## Evidence (verified this run, main `cffd524f`)

`pm-stack/aggregator/src/aa.ts`, `num()` (lines 26–37):

```ts
// The Supabase Management API serializes numeric columns as JSON
// strings, so every number crosses a coercion gate here; anything
// unparseable becomes null ("not measured"), never 0.

export function num(v: unknown): number | null {
  if (typeof v === "number") return Number.isFinite(v) ? v : null;
  if (typeof v === "string" && v.trim() !== "") {
    const n = Number(v);
    return Number.isFinite(n) ? n : null;
  }
  return null;
}
```

`num()` is the single coercion gate for every AA figure the aggregator renders: Test_Runs sheet fields (`raw_score` → intelligence, `cost_per_task_usd`, `output_speed_tps`, `ttft_seconds`, … at aa.ts:96–102), workbook rankings/thresholds (aa.ts:917–956), and catalog context/output limits (aa.ts:878–879). Its header contract promises that anything unparseable becomes `null` ("not measured"). But bare `Number()` accepts radix-prefixed and whitespace-padded spellings that neither Postgres numeric serialization (the Supabase Management API's format) nor a workbook cell produces — so a drifted value is silently reinterpreted into a *different measured number* instead of reading as not-measured.

