| `"0b101"` | **5** (binary reinterpreted) |
| `"0o17"` | **15** (octal reinterpreted) |
| `"1e2"` | **100** (scientific reinterpreted) |
| `"  12  "` | **12** (whitespace-padded accepted) |
| controls: `"12"`, `"12.5"` | 12, 12.5 (correct) |
| controls: `""`, `"   "`, `"abc"`, `"12abc"`, `"Infinity"`, `"1,200"`, `42`, `NaN`, `null` | null / null / null / null / null / null / 42 / null / null (correct) |

No test in `aggregator/test/aa.test.ts` exercises `num()` directly.

## Why it matters

This is the same reinterpretation class already filed and fixed for sibling gates — meta-exporter's `num()` (#2789) and cloudflare-export's `num()` (#2791) — but the aggregator's AA gate was never covered. The AA section feeds model rankings, cost-per-task and speed figures that the dashboard presents as measured data. A shape-drifted upstream string (the exact failure this gate exists to absorb, per its own header) currently publishes as a plausible wrong measurement — e.g. a drifted `raw_score` of `"0x10"` renders as intelligence 16 — instead of the section's honest "not measured" null.

## Acceptance criteria

- `num()` returns `null` for radix-prefixed string spellings (`0x…`, `0b…`, `0o…`, any case) at every call site — sheet fields, rankings/thresholds, and catalog limits.
