That matters because of how `main` ends (:963): it catches `(SafetyError, OSError, CalledProcessError, TimeoutExpired, SystemExit)`, writes the `refused` run record to the state-dir log, and exits 2. The comment there records the #6812 lesson verbatim: a refusal is a run too — until it left a record, an hourly-dead janitor was visible only in the systemd journal. A malformed scan document or broker row raises `AttributeError`/`KeyError`/`TypeError`/`ValueError` instead, which is NOT in that tuple: the process dies with a raw traceback, no `refused` record is written, and the janitor is hourly-dead invisibly again — the exact failure mode #6812 fixed, through a different door. (The crash still happens before any deletion, so this is an observability/robustness defect, not a wrongful-deletion one.)

