## Evidence
In `pkgs/id/crates/id-cli/src/publications_runtime.rs:225-234`:

```rust
if let Err(error) = self.mounts.mount(&publication.world, path) {
    // The registry is already persisted with the
    // mount in it; revert the persisted set so the
    // two never silently disagree, and say so.
    let mut reverted = self.registry.clone();
    let _ = reverted.unpublish(&publication.placement);
    let _ = reverted.save(&self.registry_path);
    self.registry = reverted;
    return Err(error).context("applying the path mount after persisting it");
}
```

Both `reverted.unpublish(...)` and `reverted.save(&self.registry_path)` discard their `Result`s with `let _ =`, and `self.registry = reverted;` unconditionally updates the in-memory registry.

## Why it matters
This directly violates the adjacent comment's invariant (`revert the persisted set so the two never silently disagree, and say so`): if `save` fails, the on-disk registry retains the failed mount while `self.registry` in memory has it removed, and the caller is never informed.

## Acceptance criteria
- Check `reverted.unpublish(...)` and `reverted.save(&self.registry_path)`; if either fails, attach the revert error to the returned error and do not overwrite `self.registry` on `save` failure.
- Add a unit test verifying that a `save` failure during path-mount rollback is surfaced in the returned error and does not desync `self.registry`.

