## Problem

`GET /items?refs=` and `GET /comments?refs=` split the query param into an unbounded array and hand it straight to the store, where it becomes one SQL placeholder per ref. Both routes are unauthenticated reads, and the `refs` path bypasses the `limit` pagination cap that the same routes enforce on their list path. Additionally, the by-refs items query has no row `LIMIT` at all.

## Evidence (origin/main `ec68de7f`)

`pm-stack/projector/src/server.ts:292-309` — `GET /items`: no auth gate (first `bearerAuthorized` in the file is at :336 for `POST /links`), no count cap on `refs`:

```
if (path === "/items" && method === "GET") {
  const refsParam = url.searchParams.get("refs");
  if (refsParam) {
    const refs = refsParam
      .split(",")
      .map((s) => s.trim())
      .filter(Boolean);
    const rows = await store.getItems(db, refs);
    return jsonResponse(200, { items: rows });
  }
  ... limit: parseLimit(..., DEFAULT_ITEMS_LIMIT, MAX_ITEMS_LIMIT),
```

`pm-stack/projector/src/server.ts:312-325` — `GET /comments`: same uncapped split, passed to `store.getComments(db, refs, { limit })`.

`pm-stack/projector/src/store.ts:247-254` — `getItems` builds `IN (?, ?, ...)` with one placeholder per ref and has **no `LIMIT` clause**:

```
export async function getItems(db: Client, refs: string[]): Promise<ItemRow[]> {
  if (refs.length === 0) return [];
  const placeholders = refs.map(() => "?").join(",");
  const r = await db.execute({
    sql: `SELECT * FROM pmp_items WHERE ref IN (${placeholders}) ORDER BY updated_at DESC`,
    args: refs,
  });
```

`pm-stack/projector/src/store.ts:427-441` — `getComments` caps returned rows via `LIMIT ?` but still builds an unbounded placeholder list.

The sibling read routes cap page size (`MAX_ITEMS_LIMIT = MAX_COMMENTS_LIMIT = 1000`, server.ts:78-81, enforced by `parseLimit`), so a caller paging normally can never pull more than 1000 rows — but the `refs` path on the same endpoints has no equivalent bound.

## Why it matters

An unauthenticated caller can request an arbitrarily large ref list (hundreds of short refs fit in a normal URL) and force a giant `IN` query returning up to one full row per ref on `/items` with no row cap — response bloat and DB load on the shared projector store. At very high counts it also risks hitting the SQLite host-variable limit, which would surface through the unguarded-store-call path already tracked in #10540 as an unstructured 500.

## Suggested fix shape

- Cap the `refs` array length on both routes (e.g. reuse `MAX_ITEMS_LIMIT`/`MAX_COMMENTS_LIMIT` or a dedicated `MAX_REFS`), returning `400 { error }` over the cap.
- Add a row `LIMIT` to `getItems`-by-refs consistent with the list path.
- Related, not duplicates: #10540 (structured-500 error boundary — this issue is the input guard that keeps the failure from happening), #6622 (repo-wide pagination-contract epic — this is the concrete instance for these two routes).

## Evidence

`journal_backup` — `pkgs/id/crates/id-cli/src/commands/world/journal.rs:169-190` (verified on origin/main 45191b4e2e14910a0365537f22014daa1a60daf0):

```rust
if out.exists() {
    bail!("backup output {} already exists", out.display());
}
std::fs::write(out, &bytes).with_context(|| format!("write {}", out.display()))?;
let written = std::fs::read(out).with_context(|| format!("read back {}", out.display()))?;
if written != bytes {
    bail!(
        "backup verification failed: {} does not match {} byte for byte",
        out.display(),
        path.display()
    );
}
```

`std::fs::write` creates/truncates and can fail partway (ENOSPC, interrupted write); the read-back verify exists precisely to catch that. But on either failure the command bails **without removing the partial/corrupt `out`**. The next run hits the no-overwrite guard and fails with "backup output ... already exists", so the chosen backup path is stuck until an operator deletes the corrupt file by hand.

## Why it matters

The conservative exists-guard (a backup never overwrites an earlier one) ends up protecting the corrupt file the command itself just wrote, instead of the earlier backup it was designed for. A transient write failure becomes a persistent operator-visible bad state, and the natural retry (same command, same path) is refused.

## Acceptance criteria

- On failure after `out` was created (write error or verification mismatch), the partial file is removed; if the removal itself fails, that error is appended to the failure message rather than turned into success.
- The no-overwrite guard still applies to files this invocation did not write.
- A test covers the injected-failure case: a failed backup leaves no `out` behind, and a pre-existing unrelated file is still refused.

## Dedupe

All-state searches for "journal backup" surface #10821/#11085/#11564 (the feature) and #2093 (check-vs-open disagreement) — none cover partial-output cleanup on backup failure.

