## Evidence

`NodeStore::flush_index` — `pkgs/id/crates/id-sync/src/federation/record_store.rs:665-678` — is the single commit point for every write-through (`StoredReplica::produce`, `receive`, `reconcile_with`, `reconcile_remote_with`, and `ServingNode::persist_session`). It writes a tmp file, syncs the file, renames, and returns `Ok`:

```rust
fn flush_index(&self) -> Result<(), RecordStoreError> {
    let tmp_path = self.index_path.with_extension("tmp");
    let mut file = fs::File::create(&tmp_path)?;
    file.write_all(&self.index.encode())?;
    file.sync_all()?;
    drop(file);
    fs::rename(&tmp_path, &self.index_path)?;
    Ok(())
}
```

The parent directory is never synced after the rename. `id_core::durable_write::write_durable` (`pkgs/id/crates/id-core/src/durable_write.rs:46-70`) is the project's established durable-replacement contract — tmp write, file sync, rename, **parent-dir sync**, with a `WriteStage::AfterRename` error so callers can reconcile — and this commit point does not use it. Verified on origin/main 45191b4e2e14910a0365537f22014daa1a60daf0.

## Why it matters

The module's own contract (record_store.rs:29-46) is that once a write-through point commits, a crash leaves "the old index or the new one". Without a directory fsync the rename may not survive a crash/power loss: the node comes back with the **old** index even though `flush_index` already returned `Ok`. For versions admitted by exchange that is tolerable by design — anti-entropy re-sends them. But for a **locally produced** version (`StoredReplica::produce`, record_store.rs:1043-1060) no peer re-offers it: the producer was told the admission committed (the CLI printed the record's address), and after a crash the version is silently absent from replay. The same holds for a `ServingNode` session whose initiator is gone. `Ok` from `flush_index` is the durability promise callers act on; today it is not a durability promise at all.

## Suggested fix shape

Route `flush_index` through `id_core::durable_write::write_durable` (or add the parent-dir sync plus an after-rename error stage in the same shape), matching every other id state-file writer. A unit test can inject a parent-sync failure exactly as `durable_write.rs:100-115` does and check the caller surfaces the stage instead of reporting success.

## Acceptance criteria

- `flush_index` syncs the node directory after the rename (or delegates to `write_durable`).
- A parent-dir sync failure after rename is reported to the caller, never swallowed into `Ok`.
- Staging/rollback semantics of the write-through points are otherwise unchanged.
- A test covers the after-rename failure path.

## Dedupe

#12492 (closed) introduced the record store but does not cover index dirsync; #12152/#12254 (closed) fixed other writers' durability; fresh all-state searches for "flush_index", "record store index durability", "directory fsync" found no open or closed match.
